+## Contextual examples throughout play
+
+A writing aid should remain available after onboarding wherever the game actually supports free input. First inventory real input surfaces (conversation, action description, or room requests when implemented); do not add unsupported input or buttons to output-only panels just to claim global coverage.
+
+- Keep a discoverable “Give an example / 举个例子” near the input, with a way to cycle through a few short, distinct suggestions. Use the game's normal responsive controls and locale system. This is a local writing aid, not an extra model request, turn, reward, or automatic submission. Filling a draft is not a successful AI exchange.
+- Keep example content in the game-owned authoring adapter. Resolve from the current speaker/place, introduced information, completed tasks and supported actions. Completion outranks unconsumed topics or consumed items. A character being known does not make every fact known: check the actual introduction, presented reply or discovered source. Player knowledge of a generated room does not prove that its observations are present in that NPC's model context. Use a neutral question when context is missing rather than implying hidden facts or unsupported actions.
+- Respect the existing reply-reading gate. Show suggestions when the next input is available, not midway through an unread reply. During an in-flight or unresolved request, prevent edits/replacement that could change the pending intent.
+- Click-to-fill leaves the text editable and requires manual send. An empty field may be filled directly. A nonempty user draft or restored draft must not be silently overwritten: preview the proposal with explicit replace/keep actions, or another clearly non-destructive pattern. Cycling may replace the helper's own unchanged insertion in the same context; once edited, protect it as a user draft.
+- Invalidate cycling, preview and ownership when journey/actor, speaker, place, locale or relevant progress changes. Preserve the user's draft using the game's existing scoped draft contract. Do not migrate story state, add completion flags or reuse a pending request ID for a different suggestion.
+- Reuse behavior separately from content: an assistance control can accept candidate IDs/text, current value, change callback, input target and disabled state; a context key resets transient UI state. Keep model submission and persistence in their existing owners. Avoid a shared template full of one game's characters, quest flags or example questions.
+
+Verify the input coverage list, first and repeat conversations, completion out of order, missing-context fallback, editing then cycling, explicit replace/keep, close/reload, changed progress, rapid activation, focus and disabled/pending behavior. Check both product languages at 320×568, 390×844 and desktop, long text and scrollable option lists. Keyboard focus or a shortened desktop viewport is not proof of native iOS keyboard behavior. Use a local intercepted transport for UI checks; examples alone must cause zero action/model requests. Test actual touch activation as well: a panel opened on pointer-down can otherwise be dismissed by the trailing click on its new backdrop. Close a backdrop only for a gesture originating on its background, while preserving close buttons and keyboard exits.
+
+The 2026-10-04 Harbor candidate verified a shared conversation control across nine NPC inputs using synthetic local authority, both locales, draft restoration, reading gates and browser touch emulation. Its room generation and authored actions have no free-text input and were explicitly excluded. This validates the UI contract in one spatial shell; it does not verify live model answer quality, platform identity, physical-device keyboards, deployment, or another shell's integration. See the game-owned `doc/qa/examples-20261004/README.md` for bounded evidence; reuse the method without copying its story content.
